#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "5888d4ce-bc45-540b-afa0-901008abfcde" :question/math-academy-id "e-21787" :question/problem "Let $F(x) = \\cos(2x)$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R$. Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $[MATH: x \\in [0,\\pi)]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x+\\pi) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n2. $f(x) = F(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n3. $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$, we have\n\n- $f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, and\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $[MATH: x\\in[0,\\pi).]$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi$-periodicity:\n$f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$\nfor all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi$-periodic, because for any $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, we have\n$F(x + π) = \\cos (2(x + π)) = \\cos (2x + 2π) = \\cos (2x) = F(x)$.\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0$. Then,\n\n$$\nf(x + \\frac{π}{2}) = f(\\frac{π}{2}) = F(\\frac{π}{2}) = \\cos (π) =-1\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\nf(x) = f(0) = F(0)\\cos (0) = 1\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nf\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\neq f(x)\n$$\n\nfor $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fadf4f64-3569-56b2-98e1-f6681892054a" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Orthogonality of Sines and Cosines"}] :question/id #uuid "052f11a0-2f27-5285-83af-6d8fdb729024" :question/math-academy-id "e-21788" :question/problem "$\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) g(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x$\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $[MATH: x\\in [0,2\\pi)]$ defined above, compute $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(6x), \\cos(4x) \\rangle$ and determine if functions $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\sin(6x)\\cos(4x) =\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\sin(10x)+\\sin(2x)\\big)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨\\sin (6x), \\cos (4x)⟩ &= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\sin (6x)\\cos (4x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (10x) + \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2x)dx \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{1}{20}\\cos (10x) - \\frac{1}{4}\\cos (2x)]_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{20}(\\cos (20π) - \\cos (0)) - \\frac{1}{4}(\\cos (4π) - \\cos (0)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{20}(1 - 1) - \\frac{1}{4}(1 - 1) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sin(6x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\cos(4x)\n$$\n\n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "d2127a8e-14bb-5574-a542-852333fe4cca" :question/math-academy-id "e-21789" :question/problem "Let $F(x) = 2x^2+5$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R$, and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $[MATH: x\\in [0,2\\pi)]$ to $x\\in \\mathbb R$. The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n$f(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx)\\right)$.\nDetermine the integral expression for the coefficient $a_4$." :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $[MATH: x\\in [0,2\\pi)]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $[MATH: x\\in [0,2\\pi),]$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0$, $a_n$, and $b_n$ are constants to be determined.\n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\cos(4x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(x), \\cos (4x)⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx)), \\cos (4x)⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨\\frac{a_{0}}{2}, \\cos (4x)⟩ + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)⟨a_{n}\\cos (nx), \\cos (4x)⟩ + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)⟨b_{n}\\sin (nx), \\cos (4x)⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{0}}{2}⟨1, \\cos (4x)⟩ + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}⟨\\cos (nx), \\cos (4x)⟩ + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}⟨\\sin (nx), \\cos (4x)⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1, \\sin(x), \\cos(x), \\sin(2x), \\cos(2x), \\ldots$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\na_4 \\left\\langle \\cos(4x), \\cos(4x) \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨\\cos (4x), \\cos (4x)⟩ &= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\cos^{2} (4x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (8x)dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{16}\\sin (8x)]_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2π - 0) - \\frac{1}{16}(\\sin (16π) - \\sin (0)) \\\\\n&= π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(x), \\cos (4x)⟩ &= a_{4}⟨\\cos (4x), \\cos (4x)⟩ \\\\\na_{4} &= (⟨f(x), \\cos (4x)⟩)/(⟨\\cos (4x), \\cos (4x)⟩) \\\\\na_{4} &= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{0}^{2π}f(x)\\cos (4x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π} \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2π}(2x^{2} + 5)\\cos (4x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/a